#{[17592186555799 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555799 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555799 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555799 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=200$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,200.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{200}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555799 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "490b7df2-1c00-5889-8265-81d6b29b18f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555799 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60%=0.6,\\,n=200.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(200,0.6).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555810 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,80.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{80}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59f1c663-db33-5cbd-a122-ef9383054d3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555810 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=72%=0.72,\\,n=80.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(80,0.72).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555835 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555835 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555835 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555835 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=120$ from a population where $5%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,120.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{120}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94f8387a-ce74-5bb3-92d4-c497c3ceea9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555835 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=5%=0.05,\\,n=120.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(120,0.05)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05\\cdot (1-0.05) \\\\  & =5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555845 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555845 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555845 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555845 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $38%$ of a city's adult population like romance movies. A random sample of $n=30$ adults is taken from the population and asked about their favorite movie genre.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of people in the sample who like romance movies? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555845 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a90f2432-beab-5d93-b930-00f3398843bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555845 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=38%=0.38,$ and $n=30.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =38%=0.38, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{30} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555879 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555879 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0eb51c85-c2ef-507e-b1a1-aca1befe52a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555879 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ded7ce8-7b5b-5a82-bc65-9550d172352f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "416e8b5c-3b97-5021-9133-e508e5a83961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555915 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "774983eb-fe83-513b-8f77-ab281c6af635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6662e34-cc38-5218-b94a-784dde3dea35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555845 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
